## Context

oh-my-byted exposes host capabilities to third-party widgets. Some of them should only run in response to the user, e.g. `omb.open` (switch the user to another tool) — similar to how browsers gate `window.open` / clipboard writes behind user activation.

Today a `CapabilityHandler` only sees `(method, input)`:

```rust
fn call(&mut self, method: &str, input: UiValue) -> Result<UiValue, String>;
```

so the host cannot tell whether a call comes from a pointer/key handler, a `timeout`, a `start_task` completion, a subscription push, or an effect. We tried inferring it by time ("was there any window input in the last 1.5 s?"). That is a heuristic with real problems:

- **False positives**: any click anywhere unlocks every view's timers for the window.
- **No attribution**: it can't tell which view the user interacted with.
- **Coupled to the host's element tree**: our first version listened with a root `capture_any_mouse_down`, which never fires for clicks inside `occlude()`d overlays because element listeners require `hitbox.is_hovered` (`div.rs`). Users saw "clicking a row does nothing, but press ↓ first and then it works". Switching to `window.on_mouse_event` from a zero-size canvas fixes that, but it's still a guess.
- **Tests have to fake input**, because `AutomationCommand::Dispatch` calls handlers directly.

We are dropping the gate on our side for now (rate limit + call log instead). This issue is about giving hosts the precise signal so gating can be done properly later.

## Ask

Pass the invocation origin to capability handlers, e.g.

```rust
pub enum InvocationOrigin {
    UserInput { event: &'static str },   // pointer / key / click dispatched from a platform input event
    Automation,                          // AutomationCommand::Dispatch
    Timer, TaskCompletion, Subscription, Effect, Render,
}

pub trait CapabilityHandler {
    fn call(&mut self, method: &str, input: UiValue) -> Result<UiValue, String>;
    /// Default forwards to `call`, so existing handlers keep compiling.
    fn call_with(&mut self, origin: &InvocationOrigin, method: &str, input: UiValue)
        -> Result<UiValue, String> { self.call(method, input) }
}
```

The runtime already knows which path it is on when it invokes a script callback; synchronous helper calls inside the same dispatch keep the same origin. A handler that starts a task and calls the capability in the task callback gets `TaskCompletion` (that is the point).

**Performance**: this should be an enum set on the dispatch path (a field on the existing invocation / context struct, or a stack slot restored on return) and read only by handlers that ask for it — no allocation, no locking, nothing per frame. If it can't be done without measurable cost on the event / render path, we're fine without it.

Also useful (optional): let the host see which view the event was dispatched to, so activation can be attributed per view.

## Summary

In **0.1.8**, a `Table` with column `adornments` fails with `duplicate component instance path` as soon as its virtual body realizes new rows next to rows retained from the previous batch. The simplest trigger is scrolling. After the error the view stays in its error state. 0.1.7 passes the same scenario.

```
failed to evaluate Rhai UI source: Runtime error: duplicate component instance path
`/View[adorned-table-view]/Table[tce-table]/VirtualCollection[tce-table-body]/Badge[components/badge@line 50, position 19#0]`;
add a stable key @ 'components/badge' (line 50, position 19)
```

## How to reproduce

1. Check out `v0.1.8`.
2. Save the test below as `tests/native-keyboard/tests/adornment_virtual_rows.rs`. It is self-contained: it reads `registry/components/{table,badge}.rhai`, the default dark theme, the `en` locale and the bundled icons from the repository.
3. Run:
   ```
   cargo test --manifest-path tests/native-keyboard/Cargo.toml --test adornment_virtual_rows -- --nocapture
   ```

The test mounts a 60-row Table that has one adorned column and a fixed 200px height. It records which rows are realized, according to the accessibility snapshot. Then it scrolls the body by 120px with a `ScrollWheelEvent` and asserts two things: the scroll realized new rows while keeping some previous ones, and `last_error()` is still `None`.

| source | realized rows before → after | result |
|---|---|---|
| 0.1.7 (same test, same tree layout) | (0, 6) → (4, 10) | passes |
| `v0.1.8` | (0, 6) → (4, 10) | **fails**: `left: Some("… duplicate component instance path …/Badge[components/badge@line 50, position 19#0] …")` |
| `v0.1.8` plus the change suggested below | (0, 6) → (4, 10) | passes |

The test was run from local scratch copies of the tags. For reference, the same file lives at `~/.cache/gpui-rhai-018/tests/native-keyboard/tests/adornment_virtual_rows.rs` on the reporter's machine.

<details><summary><code>tests/native-keyboard/tests/adornment_virtual_rows.rs</code></summary>

```rust
// Regression test for duplicate component instance paths of Table adornment badges
// when the virtual body realizes new rows next to retained ones (0.1.8).
// Place at tests/native-keyboard/tests/adornment_virtual_rows.rs and run:
//   cargo test --manifest-path tests/native-keyboard/Cargo.toml --test adornment_virtual_rows -- --nocapture
use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::collections::BTreeMap;
use std::rc::Rc;

use gpui::{
    Context, IntoElement, Modifiers, Render, ScrollDelta, ScrollWheelEvent, TestAppContext,
    VisualTestContext, Window, point, px,
};
use gpui_rhai::*;

const MAIN: &str = r#"
import "components/table" as table;
fn view(ctx) {
    let rows = [];
    for i in 0..60 {
        rows.push(#{ id: `r${i}`, name: `cluster-${i}`, state: "running", variant: "success" });
    }
    table::Table(#{ key: "tce-table", label: "Clusters", row_key: "id", rows: rows,
        columns: [
            #{ key: "name", title: "Cluster", width: #{ kind: "flex", value: 1.0 },
                adornments: [#{ text_key: "state", variant_key: "variant", dot: true }] },
        ],
        height: 200 })
}
"#;

struct Host {
    host: ScriptViewHost,
    view: ScriptViewHandle,
}

impl Render for Host {
    fn render(&mut self, _: &mut Window, _: &mut Context<Self>) -> impl IntoElement {
        self.host.container(self.view.element().unwrap())
    }
}

fn realized_rows(visual: &mut VisualTestContext, view: &ScriptViewHandle) -> (usize, usize) {
    let indexes: Vec<usize> = visual.update(|_, cx| {
        view.accessibility_snapshot(cx)
            .unwrap()
            .nodes()
            .filter_map(|node| {
                let at = node.name.find("cluster-")?;
                node.name[at + 8..]
                    .split(|c: char| !c.is_ascii_digit())
                    .next()?
                    .parse()
                    .ok()
            })
            .collect()
    });
    (
        indexes.iter().copied().min().unwrap_or(0),
        indexes.iter().copied().max().unwrap_or(0),
    )
}

#[gpui::test]
fn scrolling_an_adorned_table_realizes_new_rows_without_duplicate_paths(cx: &mut TestAppContext) {
    cx.update(gpui_rhai::install);
    let registry = concat!(env!("CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR"), "/../../registry");
    let read = |path: &str| std::fs::read_to_string(format!("{registry}/{path}")).unwrap();
    let entry = ModuleId::parse("main").unwrap();
    let mut modules = BTreeMap::from([(entry.clone(), MAIN.to_owned())]);
    for name in ["table", "badge"] {
        modules.insert(
            ModuleId::parse(&format!("components/{name}")).unwrap(),
            read(&format!("components/{name}.rhai")),
        );
    }
    let mut assets = Vec::new();
    for file in std::fs::read_dir(format!("{registry}/assets/icons")).unwrap() {
        let path = file.unwrap().path();
        let stem = path.file_stem().unwrap().to_string_lossy().to_string();
        assets.push((
            format!("icons/{stem}"),
            AssetData {
                mime_type: "image/svg+xml".to_owned(),
                bytes: std::fs::read(&path).unwrap(),
            },
        ));
    }
    let prepared = EmbeddedScriptView::new(
        entry,
        EmbeddedScriptSource::new(modules),
        &read("themes/default_dark.rhai"),
    )
    .asset_sources(assets)
    .locale_sources([("en".to_owned(), read("locales/en.rhai"))])
    .prepare()
    .unwrap();
    let captured = Rc::new(RefCell::new(None));
    let capture = captured.clone();
    let window = cx.add_window(move |window, cx| {
        let host = ScriptViewHost::new("adorned-table", cx).unwrap();
        let view = prepared
            .mount(ScriptViewConfig::new("adorned-table-view"), host.clone(), window, cx)
            .unwrap();
        *capture.borrow_mut() = Some(view.clone());
        Host { host, view }
    });
    cx.run_until_parked();
    cx.refresh().unwrap();
    cx.run_until_parked();
    let view = captured.borrow().as_ref().unwrap().clone();
    let mut visual = VisualTestContext::from_window(*window, cx);
    visual.run_until_parked();
    let before = realized_rows(&mut visual, &view);
    assert_eq!(visual.update(|_, cx| view.last_error(cx).unwrap()), None);

    let position = point(px(200.0), px(150.0));
    visual.simulate_mouse_move(position, None, Modifiers::none());
    visual.simulate_event(ScrollWheelEvent {
        position,
        delta: ScrollDelta::Pixels(point(px(0.0), px(-120.0))),
        ..Default::default()
    });
    visual.run_until_parked();
    visual.update(|window, _| window.refresh());
    visual.run_until_parked();
    let after = realized_rows(&mut visual, &view);
    println!("realized rows before {before:?}, after {after:?}");
    // The scroll must realize new rows while keeping some of the previous ones.
    assert!(after.0 > before.0 && after.0 <= before.1 && after.1 > before.1);
    assert_eq!(visual.update(|_, cx| view.last_error(cx).unwrap()), None);
}
```

</details>

### Seen in a real app

oh-my-byted mounts several adorned cluster tables in one view. It first renders cached rows and then swaps in freshly fetched rows, with no scrolling involved. On 0.1.8 this failed in 2 of 3 runs with the same error. On 0.1.7 it passed all 3 runs with the same data and cache.

## Analysis

- `render_table_row` (`registry/components/table.rhai`) creates each adornment badge without a `key` prop. It calls `.with_key(...)` on the returned node, which does not take part in the component instance path:
  ```rhai
  content.push(badge::Badge(#{ text: adornment.text, variant: adornment.variant,
      dot: adornment.dot, size: "sm" }).with_key(`${cell.key}-adornment-${index}`));
  ```
- A keyless formal component gets the automatic key `callsite#occurrence`, with the occurrence counted in `active.positions` per parent and call site (`engine.rs` `reserve_component_path`, around L3050). All rows share the parent `VirtualCollection[tce-table-body]`, so their badges are told apart only by that counter.
- `retain_virtual_component_manifest` (`engine.rs`, around L2785) adds the retained rows' paths to `active.seen` but does not advance `active.positions`. The first newly realized row therefore numbers its badge `#0` again, which equals a retained row's path, and `reserve_component_path` rejects it.

From this reading, any keyless formal component inside a `virtual_collection` item renderer can hit the same collision. We have not tested renderers other than Table.

## Suggestion (for the maintainers to decide)

This is only what we tried locally. Please choose whatever fix fits the runtime design.

- **Registry-level.** The single change that made the test above and the oh-my-byted scenario pass was a row-scoped `key` prop on the badge. With it, that app's full smoke run also passed: 17 panels and 14 resident views, 0 failures.
  ```rhai
  content.push(badge::Badge(#{ key: `${payload.key}-${cell.key}-adornment-${index}`,
      text: adornment.text, variant: adornment.variant,
      dot: adornment.dot, size: "sm" }).with_key(`${cell.key}-adornment-${index}`));
  ```
- **Runtime-level ideas** (not tried). Automatic `callsite#occurrence` keys may not be a stable identity inside virtual items, since the same row can receive a different occurrence number in different batches. Possible directions:
  - give each item render its own path segment, for example `VirtualCollection[body]/Item[<row key>]/…`;
  - or report keyless formal components inside item renderers with an explicit diagnostic.

## Summary

`ScriptViewHandle::composition_audit` does not look inside `error_boundary(...)`: content rendered through a boundary is never audited. A host that wraps each embedded widget in an error boundary gets 0 findings no matter what the widgets contain.

Found while moving oh-my-byted to `feat/design-system-0.2` (c28849a). It wraps every widget of a panel in `error_boundary` so that one failing widget doesn't take down the others. With the productivity profile, its smoke run reported 0 findings for every panel, even after we deliberately added a row with two primary buttons and mixed type sizes. With the boundary removed, the same panels report 13 findings in the demo configuration and 35 in a second configuration, and the deliberate violations show up as `multiple-solid-actions` and `mixed-type-in-row`.

## How to reproduce

1. Check out `feat/design-system-0.2` (c28849a).
2. Save the test below as `tests/native-keyboard/tests/audit_error_boundary.rs`. It only needs the default dark theme and the token base from the repository.
3. Run:
   ```
   cargo test --manifest-path tests/native-keyboard/Cargo.toml --test audit_error_boundary -- --nocapture
   ```

Result on c28849a:

| test | findings | result |
|---|---|---|
| broken row directly in the view | `["mixed-type-in-row at root/0"]` | passes |
| the same row inside `error_boundary(row, text("fallback"))` | `[]` | **fails** |

The reporter's local copy of the test is at `~/.cache/gpui-rhai-020/tests/native-keyboard/tests/audit_error_boundary.rs`.

<details><summary><code>tests/native-keyboard/tests/audit_error_boundary.rs</code></summary>

```rust
// Regression test: the composition audit must see content rendered inside error_boundary.
// Place at tests/native-keyboard/tests/audit_error_boundary.rs and run:
//   cargo test --manifest-path tests/native-keyboard/Cargo.toml --test audit_error_boundary -- --nocapture
use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::collections::BTreeMap;
use std::rc::Rc;

use gpui::{Context, IntoElement, Render, TestAppContext, VisualTestContext, Window, WindowHandle};
use gpui_rhai::*;

const DEFAULT_DARK: &str = include_str!("../../../registry/themes/default_dark.rhai");
const PROFILE: &str = r#"fn profile() { #{ name: "test", audit: ["mixed-type-in-row"] } }"#;
// One row mixing a title and a caption: `mixed-type-in-row`.
const BROKEN: &str = r#"row([
    text("Big").with_style(style().typography("title")),
    text("small").with_style(style().typography("caption")),
])"#;

struct Host {
    host: ScriptViewHost,
    view: ScriptViewHandle,
}

impl Render for Host {
    fn render(&mut self, _: &mut Window, _: &mut Context<Self>) -> impl IntoElement {
        self.host.container(self.view.element().unwrap())
    }
}

fn audit(cx: &mut TestAppContext, view_body: &str) -> Vec<String> {
    cx.update(gpui_rhai::install);
    let entry = ModuleId::parse("main").unwrap();
    let main = format!("fn view(ctx) {{ column([{view_body}]) }}");
    let prepared = EmbeddedScriptView::new(
        entry.clone(),
        EmbeddedScriptSource::new(BTreeMap::from([(entry, main)])),
        DEFAULT_DARK,
    )
    .token_base(gpui_rhai_registry::TOKEN_BASE_SOURCE)
    .profile_source(PROFILE)
    .motion_preference(MotionPreference::None)
    .prepare()
    .unwrap();
    let captured = Rc::new(RefCell::new(None));
    let capture = captured.clone();
    let window: WindowHandle<Host> = cx.add_window(move |window, cx| {
        let host = ScriptViewHost::new("audit", cx).unwrap();
        let view = prepared
            .mount(ScriptViewConfig::new("audit"), host.clone(), window, cx)
            .unwrap();
        *capture.borrow_mut() = Some(view.clone());
        Host { host, view }
    });
    cx.run_until_parked();
    let view = captured.borrow().clone().unwrap();
    let mut visual = VisualTestContext::from_window(*window, cx);
    visual.run_until_parked();
    visual
        .update(|_, cx| view.composition_audit(cx).unwrap())
        .into_iter()
        .map(|finding| format!("{} at {}", finding.rule.id(), finding.path))
        .collect()
}

#[gpui::test]
fn the_audit_reports_a_broken_row_directly_in_the_view(cx: &mut TestAppContext) {
    let found = audit(cx, BROKEN);
    println!("plain: {found:?}");
    assert_eq!(found, ["mixed-type-in-row at root/0"]);
}

#[gpui::test]
fn the_audit_reports_a_broken_row_inside_an_error_boundary(cx: &mut TestAppContext) {
    let found = audit(cx, &format!(r#"error_boundary({BROKEN}, text("fallback"))"#));
    println!("error_boundary: {found:?}");
    assert_eq!(found.len(), 1, "{found:?}");
}
```

</details>

## Analysis

`Walker::visit` in `crates/gpui-rhai/src/composition_audit.rs` only descends into `Box`, `Fragment` and `Overlay` (trigger and content). Every other node kind goes to `_ => {}`, so the walk stops at an `ErrorBoundary`. From reading the code, `Layer` content and the realized items of a `VirtualCollection` also land in that branch. We only tested `ErrorBoundary`: an overlay trigger is audited (`root/0/trigger`).

The Gallery and the examples pass the audit, but it would help to state, or test, which subtrees that claim covers.

## Suggestion (for the maintainers to decide)

This is only what we tried locally. A walk that follows the rendered child of a boundary made the test above pass:

```rust
UiNodeKind::ErrorBoundary { child, .. } => {
    // The child is what renders while it succeeds; audit it in place.
    let child_id = id.and_then(|id| {
        self.inputs.tree.node(id)?.children().next().map(crate::RetainedChildLink::node)
    });
    let child = self.visit(child, child_id, scope, &format!("{path}/child"));
    inherit_first(&mut summary, &[child], role.as_deref());
}
```

With this change, the existing `composition_audit`, `example_audit`, `l2_patterns` and `gallery_acceptance` native suites still pass (19 tests). Things we did not check:
- which retained child is linked while the boundary shows its fallback;
- whether `Layer` and `VirtualCollection` content should be audited the same way, for example only the realized rows of a list.

## Summary

With `group_by` and `sticky_group_headers: true`, when the next group header reaches the top of the body and pushes the sticky one out, the sticky header slides **above** the body and paints over the column header row.

`virtual_list_element.rs` places the sticky header as an absolute child of the virtual-list root (`.relative()`, line 363) at `.top(sticky.offset)` (line 379), where `sticky_push_offset` (line 450) returns a value in `[-height, 0]`. Nothing clips that root, and it paints after the column header (its sibling above), so the pushed part covers the column header.

Found while moving oh-my-byted to 0.2: a grouped cluster table, scrolling up through the groups, shows each group header briefly covering the column titles.

## How to reproduce

1. Check out `feat/design-system-0.2` (c28849a).
2. Save the test below as `tests/native-keyboard/tests/table_sticky_group_header.rs`.
3. Run:
   ```
   cargo test --manifest-path tests/native-keyboard/Cargo.toml --test table_sticky_group_header -- --nocapture
   ```

The reporter's local copy is at `~/.cache/gpui-rhai-020/tests/native-keyboard/tests/table_sticky_group_header.rs`.

Result on c28849a: **fails**. With group B's header 10px below the body top, the sticky A header is at y=76 while the column header ends at y=97.5 (22px of overlap):

```
group header A, 8 paints at y=76, above the column header bottom 97.5: [("A, 8", 76.0, 32.0), ("B, 8", 108.0, 32.0)]
```

Note: the accessibility geometry is unclipped, so a fix that clips (rather than repositions) would need the test to check painted output instead; the failing case still shows where the header is laid out.

## Suggested fix (for the maintainers to decide)

- Clip the virtual-list root (or the body wrapper) to the list viewport, so the pushed header is cut at the body's top edge, or
- paint the sticky header inside a content mask limited to the list viewport.

oh-my-byted works around it with `part_styles: #{ body: style().clip() }` on the Table (not visually verified yet).

<details><summary><code>tests/native-keyboard/tests/table_sticky_group_header.rs</code></summary>

```rust
// Table sticky group headers: while the next group header pushes the sticky one out, the
// sticky header slides above the body (top in [-height, 0]) and paints over the column
// header row above it. Found while moving oh-my-byted to 0.2 (c28849a).
// Place at tests/native-keyboard/tests/table_sticky_group_header.rs and run:
//   cargo test --manifest-path tests/native-keyboard/Cargo.toml --test table_sticky_group_header -- --nocapture

use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::collections::BTreeMap;
use std::rc::Rc;

use gpui::{
    Context, IntoElement, Render, TestAppContext, VisualTestContext, Window, point, px,
};
use gpui_rhai::*;
use gpui_rhai_registry::{
    BUNDLED_ASSET_SOURCES, BUNDLED_COMPONENT_SOURCES_BY_ID, DEFAULT_THEME, TOKEN_BASE_SOURCE,
};

struct Host {
    host: ScriptViewHost,
    view: ScriptViewHandle,
}

impl Render for Host {
    fn render(&mut self, _: &mut Window, _: &mut Context<Self>) -> impl IntoElement {
        self.host.container(self.view.element().unwrap())
    }
}

const MAIN: &str = r#"
import "components/table" as table;
fn view(ctx) {
    let rows = [];
    for g in ["A", "B", "C"] {
        for i in 0..8 { rows.push(#{ id: `${g}-${i}`, g: g, name: `${g} row ${i}` }); }
    }
    column([
        text("Above the table").accessibility_role("status"),
        table::Table(#{ key: "t", label: "Grouped", row_key: "id", rows: rows,
            columns: [#{ key: "name", title: "Name", width: #{ kind: "flex", value: 1.0 } },
                #{ key: "g", title: "Group", width: #{ kind: "fixed", value: 80.0 } }],
            group_by: "g", sticky_group_headers: true, height: 200.0 }),
    ]).with_style(style().width(px(400)).padding(px(40)))
}
"#;

fn mount(cx: &mut TestAppContext, allowed: bool) -> (VisualTestContext, ScriptViewHandle) {
    cx.update(gpui_rhai::install);
    let mut modules = BTreeMap::new();
    for (id, source) in BUNDLED_COMPONENT_SOURCES_BY_ID {
        modules.insert(ModuleId::parse(*id).unwrap(), (*source).to_owned());
    }
    modules.insert(ModuleId::parse("main").unwrap(), MAIN.to_owned());
    let prepared = EmbeddedScriptView::new(
        ModuleId::parse("main").unwrap(),
        EmbeddedScriptSource::new(modules),
        DEFAULT_THEME,
    )
    .token_base(TOKEN_BASE_SOURCE)
    .asset_sources(BUNDLED_ASSET_SOURCES.iter().map(|(path, source)| {
        (
            path.strip_suffix(".svg").unwrap_or(path).to_owned(),
            AssetData {
                mime_type: "image/svg+xml".to_owned(),
                bytes: source.as_bytes().to_vec(),
            },
        )
    }))
    .prepare()
    .unwrap();
    let captured = Rc::new(RefCell::new(None));
    let capture = captured.clone();
    let window = cx.add_window(move |window, cx| {
        let host = ScriptViewHost::new("window-drag", cx).unwrap();
        let view = prepared
            .mount(
                ScriptViewConfig::new("window-drag").window_drag_areas(allowed),
                host.clone(),
                window,
                cx,
            )
            .unwrap();
        *capture.borrow_mut() = Some(view.clone());
        Host { host, view }
    });
    cx.run_until_parked();
    let mut visual = VisualTestContext::from_window(*window, cx);
    visual.update(|window, _| window.refresh());
    visual.run_until_parked();
    let view = captured.borrow().clone().unwrap();
    (visual, view)
}


fn nodes(visual: &mut VisualTestContext, view: &ScriptViewHandle) -> Vec<(String, String, GeometryBounds)> {
    let tree = visual.update(|_, cx| view.accessibility_snapshot(cx).unwrap());
    tree.nodes()
        .filter_map(|n| n.geometry.map(|g| (n.role.clone(), n.name.clone(), g.visual)))
        .collect()
}

#[gpui::test]
fn a_pushed_sticky_group_header_stays_below_the_column_header(cx: &mut TestAppContext) {
    let (mut visual, view) = mount(cx, false);
    let before = nodes(&mut visual, &view);
    let header_bottom = before
        .iter()
        .filter(|(role, _, _)| role == "columnheader")
        .map(|(_, _, b)| b.y + b.height)
        .fold(f64::MIN, f64::max);
    let first_group = before
        .iter()
        .find(|(role, name, _)| role == "rowheader" && name.starts_with("A"))
        .map(|(_, _, b)| *b)
        .expect("group A header");
    let row = first_group.height;
    // Scroll until group B's header sits 10px below the body top: the sticky A header is
    // then pushed up by row - 10. Wheel steps are applied in pieces, so step until there.
    let body_top = first_group.y;
    for _ in 0..20 {
        let now = nodes(&mut visual, &view);
        let b_top = now
            .iter()
            .find(|(role, name, _)| role == "rowheader" && name.starts_with("B"))
            .map_or(body_top + 1000.0, |(_, _, b)| b.y);
        let remaining = b_top - (body_top + 10.0);
        if remaining.abs() < 0.5 {
            break;
        }
        #[allow(clippy::cast_possible_truncation)]
        let step = remaining.min(row * 4.0) as f32;
